{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>At the mouth of two witnesses, or three witnesses</b>. If testimony is established by two, why did the verse specify three for you? To draw an analogy (gezerah shavah) from three to two: just as two are one testimony, so too three are one testimony, and they are not rendered conspiring witnesses (zomemim) unless all of them are proved zomemim — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 17:6). And likewise if one of them is found to be a relative or otherwise disqualified, the entire testimony is void, and they are all executed and pay money in the case of their being proved zomemim, for the essence of the verse is to draw an analogy from three to two in all their matters, according to the opinion of our Sages (Makkot 5b). And by way of the plain sense, the Gaon Rav Saadia said: two — are witnesses; or three — are receivers of the testimony of the two. But the verse does not mention receiving testimony, only witnesses. But it seems to me that the Gaon erred in his ruling, for testimony in capital cases is accepted only before a Sanhedrin of twenty-three. But the plain sense of the verse is to say that the condemned man shall be put to death at the mouth of two witnesses when there are no more present, or at the mouth of three if three are found there; the verse says: as you have been told and as you have heard, you shall investigate the matter thoroughly at the mouth of all the witnesses found there. Now, if we heard that he transgressed in the presence of three, we send for them and they come to court and all testify, and the same applies to a hundred; for when we hear the words of all of them, the truth becomes clear. And if there were no more present, or they went on their way and are not found there, two suffice."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:14:36.448623994Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}